# SYNOPSIS

```usage
nice [-n inc] cmd [arg ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`nice` adds <inc>, 10 unless told otherwise, to its own nice value, kept within -20 to 20, and runs <cmd> with its <arg>s in its place, found on `$PATH`.
A higher nice value is a lower priority; a negative <inc> asks for a higher one.
It is sbase's command, in `/boot/bin/posix`: the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) binds that directory before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it there.

: `-n` <inc>
  Add <inc>, from -20 to 20, instead of 10.

# SEE ALSO

renice(1), nohup(1), sched(2); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/nice.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

When the nice value cannot be read or set `nice` says so, as `nice: setpriority: ` and the error, and runs <cmd> anyway.
Its exit status is then <cmd>'s.
If <cmd> cannot be run it is reported, as `nice: execvp x: No such file or directory`, and the exit status is 127 when <cmd> was not found and 126 otherwise.
An <inc> out of range is `nice: strtonum 40: too large`, and exits 1, as a usage error does.

# BUGS

VectraOS has no nice values: the C library does not implement `getpriority` or `setpriority`, so `nice` always prints two warnings, `nice: getpriority: Function not implemented` and the same for `setpriority`, and runs <cmd> at its usual priority.
The kernel log gets a line for each, the first time, saying that the call is not implemented.
